<main id="stages-and-steps-page" class="container py-5">

    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- LEFT SIDEBAR -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/Jenkins/Views/Shared/JenkinsTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- MAIN CONTENT -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">

            <!-- PAGE TITLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h1 class="fw-bold">Stages and Steps</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    Learn the concepts, syntax containment blocks, visual milestones, and linear execution boundaries required to group and organize pipeline workflows.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">What are Stages and Steps?</h2>
                <p>
                    Inside a Declarative Jenkinsfile, execution flow is broken down into structured hierarchies to ensure scannability and clear logs. The work of a continuous automation pipeline is divided into major structural phases called <strong>Stages</strong>, which contain the individual core actions called <strong>Steps</strong>.
                </p>

                <div class="alert alert-info my-3">
                    <strong>Simple Definition:</strong> Stages act as visual milestones that group related processes (like compiling or deployment), while Steps are the individual shell commands, plugin tasks, or scripts executed inside those groups.
                </div>

                <h3 class="h4 fw-bold mt-4">Containment Structure Blocks</h3>
                <p class="mb-2">Jenkins maps out the pipeline lifecycle using explicit nested code definitions:</p>
                <ul class="mb-3">
                    <li class="mb-1"><strong>The `stages` Collection Block:</strong> A primary mandatory container wrapper that holds the chronological array list of every single processing phase in the pipeline.</li>
                    <li class="mb-1"><strong>The Individual `stage` Milestones:</strong> Explicitly named logical buckets (e.g., `stage('Run Linters')`) that isolate specific business tasks and generate dedicated tracking blocks in the Blue Ocean or Stage View UI.</li>
                    <li class="mb-1"><strong>The Nested `steps` Directive Block:</strong> An inner block container that seals and validates the granular script lines, preventing commands from running floating outside an active stage.</li>
                    <li><strong>The Core Task Steps:</strong> The atomic action commands—such as invoking shell environments via <code>sh</code>, logging strings via <code>echo</code>, or archiving files via <code>archiveArtifacts</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- KEY CONCEPTS -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Key Concepts</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Linear Stage Halting Gates</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Understanding that stages execute sequentially from top to bottom. If an individual step within any stage throws an error, the remaining steps are dropped and subsequent stages are completely blocked.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Visual Stage View Auditing</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Leveraging separate stage names to view localized metrics on the Jenkins dashboard, making it simple to trace execution times or pinpoint where a build broke.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- EXAMPLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practical Jenkins Example</h2>
                <p>The following declarative pipeline showcases how multiple distinct stages organize a build, test, and release sequence with linear step scripts:</p>
                <pre><code>pipeline {
    agent any



























    // Primary container block holding the linear workflow array
    stages {
        
        stage('Fetch and Compile Source') {
            steps {
                echo 'Step Action: Fetching commit footprint layers...'
                sh 'echo "Simulating project source compilation..."'
            }
        }

        stage('Execute Quality Controls') {
            steps {
                echo 'Step Action: Initializing static analysis linter rules...'
                echo 'Step Action: Spawning unit regression tests...'
                sh 'echo "All test files verified successfully."'
            }
        }

        stage('Publish Release Assets') {
            steps {
                echo 'Step Action: Pushing compiled binaries out to environment hubs...'
                sh 'echo "Release lifecycle wrapped up clean."'
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
                </div>

            <!-- PRACTICE -->
        <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practice Exercise: Deploy and Inspect a Multi-Stage Sequence</h2>
    <div class="concept-box">
                    <h5 class="fw-bold">Step-by-Step Task Sequence:</h5>
<ol class="ps-3 mb-0">
                        <li class="mb-2">Open the script block workspace of an active test pipeline or configure a new Pipeline item from your dashboard interface.</li>
                        <li class="mb-2">Copy-paste the complete practical multi-stage declarative code snippet provided in the example section above directly into your script text terminal window and click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
<li class="mb-2">Trigger a manual validation run path by selecting the <strong>Build Now</strong> sidebar action link.</li>
<li class="mb-2">Return to the primary status panel view of your job to observe how the <strong>Stage View</strong> table renders three independent column milestones.</li>
<li>Click the current build run's tracking number box, open <strong>Console Output</strong>, and trace how individual log steps are nested under their respective stage headers.</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>

<!-- SUMMARY -->
<div class="alert alert-info mt-4">
<h2 class="h4 fw-bold">Summary</h2>
<p class="mb-0">You have completed the <strong>Stages and Steps</strong> lesson. Continue through the syllabus to learn how to manage dynamic values and credentials inside your scripts by exploring the Environment Variables lesson.</p>
</div>


